<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Mainland Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Sri Ksetra Kingdom</h1><p class="st-meta">400 CE – 850 CE (approx.) · capital Sri Ksetra</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The largest and most powerful Pyu city, near modern Pyay, whose stupas, gold manuscripts and royal urns reveal a sophisticated Buddhist kingdom.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Sri Ksetra, meaning 'field of glory', rose on the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irrawaddy-river">Irrawaddy</a> near present-day Pyay from around the 5th century, though occupation of the site began earlier. Burmese chronicles say it was founded by princes from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tagaung-kingdom">Tagaung</a>, and its great circular wall, nearly 14 km around, made it the biggest of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/pyu-city-states">Pyu city-states</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its kings, known from inscribed funerary urns of the Vikrama dynasty, ruled a city that enclosed palaces, fields and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reservoir">reservoirs</a> within its walls. Pali texts on gold leaves found at the Khin Ba mound are among the oldest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada Buddhist</a> writings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>. Massive cylindrical stupas such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bawbawgyi-pagoda">Bawbawgyi</a> and Payagyi still stand, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> coins circulated in trade.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Sri Ksetra declined from the 8th century, perhaps as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irrawaddy-river">Irrawaddy</a> shifted and power moved north, and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/nanzhao">Nanzhao</a> raids in the 9th century finished its prominence. The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/pagan-kingdom">Pagan Kingdom</a> inherited much of its Buddhist culture. The site is part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyu-ancient-cities">Pyu Ancient Cities</a> World Heritage Site.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/sri-ksetra-kingdom.jpg" alt="Sri Ksetra Kingdom" width="264" height="583"><figcaption>Image: Hybernator · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pyu_Realm.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>400 CE – 850 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sri Ksetra</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Suriya Vikrama, Harivikrama, Sihavikrama</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Theravada Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Pyu, Pali</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/myanmar">Myanmar</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sri Ksetra, Thayekhittaya, Prome (Pyu)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=625&amp;focus=sri-ksetra-kingdom" aria-label="Sri Ksetra Kingdom on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7260 1480 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Sri Ksetra Kingdom lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7580 1736L7565 1604"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7565 1604h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7580 1736h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7580 1736h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=625&amp;focus=sri-ksetra-kingdom">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

